    <Module test_staleness_declared_no_source.py>
      A gate that is red every night stops being read.
      
      The nightly's "Validate seeded data" step exited 1 on five staleness findings.
      Two of them — learning_hub and stalled_projects — are fixture-backed BY DESIGN:
      they call mark_fixture(reason="no_live_source") because no extractor has ever
      been built for them. The gate could not tell that apart from national_budget's
      live_fetch_failed, so it failed the run for a known, accepted gap every single
      night, and a genuine regression would have landed in an already-red step.
      
      Downgrading them to WARN keeps the gap reported and keeps every real failure
      failing. The distinction is the reason the domain DECLARED, never the domain
      name — and an unrecorded reason stays a FAIL, because "we don't know why this
      fell back" is the exact state this module exists to catch.
      <Class TestClassification>
        <Function test_a_declared_absence_warns_rather_than_fails>
        <Function test_every_failure_reason_still_fails[live_fetch_failed]>
        <Function test_every_failure_reason_still_fails[parser_returned_nothing]>
        <Function test_every_failure_reason_still_fails[no_document_extracted]>
        <Function test_every_failure_reason_still_fails[live_pdf_fetch_disabled]>
        <Function test_every_failure_reason_still_fails[cbk_bulletin_unavailable]>
        <Function test_an_unrecorded_reason_still_fails>
          The bootstrap_reference_data case: 26 fixture runs, reason unknown.
        <Function test_a_mixed_domain_fails>
          One declared reason must not launder a real failure beside it.
        <Function test_the_exemption_list_is_deliberately_small>
          Widening it is a decision, not a convenience — pin it.
      <Class TestRealFindings>
        The five findings from the 2026-09-04 run, classified.
        <Function test_only_the_two_by_design_domains_are_downgraded>
        <Function test_the_gate_still_fails_the_run>
          This does NOT turn the nightly green — three real failures remain.
      <Class TestAgainstTheRealClassifier>
        The tests above mirror the rule; this one runs it.
        
        A test that reimplements the logic it checks agrees with itself by
        construction. This drives check_ingestion_freshness with fabricated
        ingestion jobs so the shipped branch is what gets exercised.
        <Function test_declared_no_source_is_warn_in_the_shipped_code>
        <Function test_a_failed_fetch_is_still_fail_in_the_shipped_code>
        <Function test_an_unrecorded_reason_is_still_fail_in_the_shipped_code>
    <Module test_staleness_gates.py>
      Freshness gates must FIRE — a check that cannot fail is not a check.
      
      These gates replace row-count floors that passed every night for months
      ("[OK] Audit Records: 27 rows (expected >= 20)") while the underlying
      tables had not gained a row in 6-11 weeks. Every test below is a positive
      control: it constructs the exact condition the nightly missed and asserts
      the gate reports FAIL.
      <Class TestTableFreshness>
        <Function test_fires_on_a_frozen_table>
          THE defect: audits frozen far beyond the OAG publication cycle.
        <Function test_passes_when_data_is_current>
        <Function test_empty_table_is_not_silently_ok>
      <Class TestIngestionFreshness>
        <Function test_fires_when_every_run_used_a_fixture>
          THE defect: national_budget processed 20 rows a night from a
          git-tracked fixture, wrote nothing, and reported [OK].
        <Function test_passes_when_publisher_was_reached>
        <Function test_audits_national_discovery_gap_is_visible_despite_live_documents[failed]>
        <Function test_audits_national_discovery_gap_is_visible_despite_live_documents[partial]>
        <Function test_audits_national_discovery_gap_is_visible_despite_live_documents[deferred]>
        <Function test_successful_empty_national_discovery_retains_live_freshness>
        <Function test_missing_national_discovery_receipt_cannot_certify_live_audits[False]>
        <Function test_missing_national_discovery_receipt_cannot_certify_live_audits[True]>
        <Function test_missing_tied_national_receipt_cannot_inherit_completed_status>
        <Function test_inconsistent_completed_discovery_receipt_is_not_fresh[zero_attempts]>
        <Function test_inconsistent_completed_discovery_receipt_is_not_fresh[failed_query]>
        <Function test_unrecorded_provenance_is_not_reported_healthy>
          Absence of evidence must never render as OK — that is the exact
          false-green this module exists to eliminate.
        <Function test_missing_domain_is_flagged>
      <Class TestEveryDomainRecordsProvenance>
        `no-silent-fallbacks`, applied to the whole registry.
        
        Seven domains recorded nothing until 2026-08-29, so
        ``check_ingestion_freshness`` reported "provenance unknown — not
        confirmed healthy" for half the pipeline every night. WARN forever is
        the same failure shape as OK forever: nobody can act on it, and a domain
        that genuinely went dark looks identical to one that was never wired up.
        
        This test walks the ACTUAL registry rather than a hand-written list, so
        a new domain added without provenance fails here instead of quietly
        joining the unknown pile.
        <Function test_registry_is_not_empty>
          Positive control for the walk itself: an empty registry would
          make every assertion below vacuous.
        <Function test_every_domain_marks_its_source_mode>
        <Function test_a_domain_that_only_marks_live_still_covers_its_fallback>
          A fetcher that marks live on success but says nothing on failure
          reports 'unknown' exactly when it matters most. Any domain with a
          fixture fallback must mark it; an intentionally withheld fetch must
          instead mark the partial failure explicitly.
        <Function test_the_gate_reads_what_the_fetchers_write>
          End-to-end on the contract itself: a recorded live mode must be
          what check_ingestion_freshness reports OK for.
      <Class TestRefusedRuns>
        The fifth mode: the domain reached a verdict of "do not publish".
        
        ``national_debt.fetcher`` raises ``DebtRegisterIncomplete`` when the IDS
        creditor pull is quarantined, because serving the fixture's external rows
        would put a 13.34T headline against the register's 12.22T. The domain
        writes nothing and the previous seed's rows stand.
        
        Before this, such a run reached no ``mark_*`` at all, recorded ``unknown``,
        and fell through to the all-fixture branch, which printed:
        
            FAIL  national_debt ingestion
                  served from a FIXTURE in all 1 recent run(s) — the publisher was
                  never successfully read (reasons: unrecorded)
        
        Right severity, false prose. Nothing was served from a fixture; nothing was
        served at all, and the reason was known. The gate must say what happened.
        <Function test_a_refused_run_is_not_described_as_a_fixture>
          THE defect. A refusal published NOTHING — not a fixture.
        <Function test_the_message_tells_the_operator_what_is_on_the_page>
          "Nothing was written" is only half the fact an operator needs; the
          other half is that the site is still serving something.
        <Function test_the_message_names_the_gate_that_refused>
          The reason slug says the register was incomplete; the DETAIL says
          which gate said so, which is the part somebody can act on.
        <Function test_an_unreasoned_refusal_still_fails_rather_than_going_quiet>
          A refusal with no recorded reason is still a refusal. It must not
          fall back to the fixture prose, and it must not go OK.
        <Function test_a_refusal_outranks_older_live_runs>
          The false green this branch has to beat.
          
          The window holds 14 days. On 2026-09-07 production's window held 8
          live national_debt runs, so the first night the refusal fires the
          ``"live" in modes`` branch would have printed
          
              [OK] national_debt ingestion: reached the publisher in 8/22 ...
          
          while the register was being refused every night. A refusal is a claim
          about NOW — what is on the page today — so it is judged on the most
          recent run, the same way supersession is (see ``_latest_run_reasons``).
        <Function test_the_count_is_honest_when_only_some_runs_refused>
          Never "all N" when it was not all N — that is the same class of
          false sentence this branch exists to remove.
        <Function test_a_live_run_after_a_refusal_is_ok_again>
          The pull recovers, the register publishes. The gate must clear —
          a branch that cannot turn off becomes a permanently red gate, which
          is how real breakages hide.
        <Function test_an_ordinary_fixture_run_still_reads_as_a_fixture>
          The neighbouring branch must be untouched: a domain that really did
          serve a git-tracked file must still say so.
      <Class TestRefusedIsASharedMode>
        <Function test_the_mode_is_defined_in_the_shared_module>
        <Function test_any_domain_can_record_a_refusal>
          No national_debt special-casing: the helper takes a domain name.
        <Function test_a_refusal_is_stale>
        <Function test_the_recorded_shape_is_what_the_cli_copies_onto_the_job_row>
          ``cli.py`` reads exactly these keys off ``freshness.get()``. If a
          refusal recorded them under different names the mode would reach the
          gate as ``None`` and land back in the unknown-provenance branch.
        <Function test_the_module_docstring_comment_counts_every_mode>
          The comment above the ContextVar undercounted the modes for the
          whole life of ``partial`` — which is how the fifth one nearly got
          missed as well. Any mode constant absent from it fails here.
    <Module test_stalled_projects_edition_gate.py>
      The stalled_projects edition gate (#230).
      
      Red when COB's listing has a newer or re-issued county BIRR than the one
      published, when the newest edition has stalled-projects captions and the run
      parsed none of them, or when parsed rows were not written. OK only when every
      fact it rests on was recorded. Imports only ``seeding.staleness`` and
      ``models``.
      
      The second half of this file is an adversarial pass's findings against the
      first version of the gate, each of which made it answer OK or WARN where it
      should have failed.
